Percent total tree canopy cover (TTCC) is the amount of tree canopy cover in a block group including the tree cover provided by public parks. Across the entire city, the 176 block groups that contain parks have a median TTCC of 10.95, while the 841 without parks have a median TTCC of 11.17. This difference is non-significant (Mann-Whitney *U* = 78897, *p* = 0.168), suggesting that parks are evenly distributed across the canopy spectrum rather than concentrated in already-green neighborhoods.

Percent private tree canopy cover (PTCC) is the tree cover in a block group excluding the tree cover provided by public parks. TTCC and PTCC were compared in the 176 block groups that contain parks to determine whether the parks significantly contribute to block group-level tree canopy cover in Phoenix. The median TTCC was 10.20%, while PTCC was higher at 10.95% . This difference was statistically significant (Wilcoxon signed-rank test, *V* = 11271, *p* \< 0.001), confirming that parks meaningfully contribute to tree canopy cover when they are present in a neighborhood.

### Regression analysis of tree canopy cover {#sec-res-regress}

@tbl-desc-stats presents descriptive statistics for the block group characteristics and outcome variables (PTCC and PTAS) used in the regression analyses. Block group population is included for reference, but it was not used as a predictor variable. Based on the OLS model, percent Hispanic population, percent Black population, and ADI rank were all negatively associated with PTCC (*p* \< 0.001; see @tbl-regress). For every 1% increase in the Hispanic population, private tree canopy cover within a block group is expected to decrease by 3.5%; a 1% increase in the population of Black residents is associated with a 7.3% decrease in PTCC; and a 1-point increase in the ADI rank is associated with an 8.5% decrease in PTCC.


